# aether-keyword-over-symbol-design-philosophy

Aether applies a language-wide policy of preferring named keywords over symbolic operators wherever symbolic forms would be ambiguous or visually confusing. This is observed across: bitwise logic (band/bor/bxor/bnot replacing &/|/^/~), control flow (fork/path/otherwise replacing if/elif/else), and function lifecycle (conduit/flow replacing def/return). The single symbolic exception — the assignment operator `<<` — uses a unique, non-conventional symbol specifically to prevent confusion with value equality (`=`). The net effect is that Aether code reads closer to prose than to symbol-dense languages. When in doubt whether Aether uses a symbol or a keyword for an operation, default to the keyword form; symbols are the designed exception.

## Procedures
- When uncertain whether Aether uses a symbol or a keyword for a given operation, default to the keyword form — symbols are the rare exception.
- Treat `<<` as the one canonical symbolic assignment operator; resist any temptation to use `=` for assignment.
- When linting Aether code, flag symbolic bitwise operators (&, |, ^, ~) as errors and replace with band/bor/bxor/bnot.

## Conventions
- Symbolic operators are minimized by language design; keyword forms are always preferred for bitwise and logical operations.
- `<<` is the sole symbolic exception and is chosen precisely because it is unambiguous — it cannot be mistaken for equality.

